{
  "evidence": "Brexit: TEXT_D would revoke Article 50 or back a referendum; TEXT_F would negotiate a close‑alignment deal and hold a confirmatory referendum. NHS/social care: TEXT_D would add 1p on Income Tax for the NHS and set mental‑health waiting‑time standards; TEXT_F would raise health spending 4.3% annually, end outsourcing, offer free prescriptions/basic dentistry, and create a National Care Service with free personal care. Policing/crime: TEXT_D commits £1bn to community policing and a public‑health approach; TEXT_F would recruit more police/PCSOs, restore neighbourhood policing, and strengthen victims’ rights. Economy/cost of living and jobs: TEXT_D proposes £130bn infrastructure, Skills Wallets, and worker protections; TEXT_F offers a £400bn investment plan, a Real Living Wage, bans zero‑hours contracts, targets a 32‑hour week, and stronger worker power. Housing: TEXT_D would build 300k homes/year incl. 100k social; TEXT_F would build over 1m social homes in a decade, introduce rent controls, and end no‑fault evictions. Climate: TEXT_D targets net‑zero by 2045 and 80% renewable electricity by 2030; TEXT_F targets most emissions cuts by 2030 with ~90% low‑carbon power and a just transition.",
  "reasoning": "Both texts cover top voter priorities (Brexit, NHS, policing, economy, housing, climate), but TEXT_F places heavier emphasis on bread‑and‑butter issues like wages, housing security, free prescriptions/dentistry, and neighbourhood policing alongside a Brexit referendum plan. TEXT_D devotes more space to constitutional reforms and EU process, which were less central to everyday voter concerns than the service, cost‑of‑living, and housing measures that are more prominent in TEXT_F.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
